 heard from the family that  this has gone on that there was a video  they requested a copy weren't Ischl II  denied and then they followed up with a  sort of formal open records request and  that's when the video was released to  them they posted it on their website and  then I posted it on the reason side a  couple days later and that's when it  went viral what are what are the the key  things that have to happen for this  trend to reverse or to disappear well I  mean the most obvious solution would be  to end the drug war and then the idea  that government has you know any  business protecting us from ourselves  the second would be to to reign in SWAT  teams to so they're only performing in  the ways that they were originally  designed to perform which is to respond  to an already violent situation to you  know respond to bank robberies and  school shootings and terrorist incidents  that kind of thing the third I think is  is to videotape all these these raids  and I think this serves a number of  important functions one is you know it  would clear up any misunderstandings or  disagreements about what happened during  the raid which would you know both  implicate officers when they do  something wrong but also indemnify them  against false claims and false  accusations the second thing I think  videotaping on the raids would do is it  would force the police officers to be  sort of adopt best standards and  practices you know they're gonna be on  their best behavior if they know  everything they do during the raid is  going to be  tape and then finally you know and this  is more of an argument toward the  general public to support this idea of  videotaping them but you know the  reaction this raid was so powerful  because people actually saw what what  happens during a very typical SWAT raid  for a drug offense and I think that the  videotaping all of them and making the  videotapes making the tapes available to  the public would bring a sort of level  of transparency to the drug war it  really forced the public to confront  what they're supporting and and really  sort of see how the drug war is carried  out on a day to day to day basis and I  think you would probably see support for  reform at least sort of moving to a less  militaristic way of enforcing the drug  laws right well Radley I want to thank  you I'm Nick Gillespie for Reason TV  we've been talking to reasons Radley  Balko about the paramilitaries a ssin of  american police
 

